INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A yarn quality report distinguishes ''single yarn tenacity'' from ''skein strength'' (breaking a coiled hank/skein of many yarn wraps simultaneously, rather than one single strand). Why does skein strength testing NOT simply equal single-strand tenacity multiplied by the number of wraps in the skein?', '["Skein strength always equals EXACTLY single-strand tenacity multiplied by wrap count, with zero deviation, making this described discrepancy technically impossible","When many yarn wraps are broken SIMULTANEOUSLY as a bundled skein, they don''t all bear load with perfectly EQUAL tension distribution — natural length/tension variation between individual wraps means some wraps bear disproportionately more load and break FIRST, before the full theoretical combined strength of all wraps is ever simultaneously realized — this uneven load-sharing effect means skein strength is typically somewhat LESS than the simple mathematical product of single-strand tenacity times wrap count, a real mechanical effect distinct from simple additive strength","Skein strength is always dramatically HIGHER than the simple mathematical product of single-strand tenacity times wrap count, the opposite of the actual relationship between these two testing approaches","Skein strength and single-strand tenacity measure completely unrelated yarn properties with zero mathematical or mechanical relationship between them, making any comparison technically meaningless"]'::jsonb, 'When many yarn wraps are broken SIMULTANEOUSLY as a bundled skein, they don''t all bear load with perfectly EQUAL tension distribution — natural length/tension variation between individual wraps means some wraps bear disproportionately more load and break FIRST, before the full theoretical combined strength of all wraps is ever simultaneously realized — this uneven load-sharing effect means skein strength is typically somewhat LESS than the simple mathematical product of single-strand tenacity times wrap count, a real mechanical effect distinct from simple additive strength', 'This is a genuine, advanced mechanics insight paralleling the earlier weak-link statistical testing principle but applied to LOAD-SHARING rather than length-based sampling — when many yarn wraps are wound into a skein and pulled simultaneously, natural small variations in each wrap''s exact length/pre-tension mean they don''t all bear the applied load with perfectly equal, simultaneous distribution — some wraps (the tightest/shortest) bear disproportionately more load and reach their individual breaking point FIRST, triggering a progressive, cascading failure rather than all wraps sharing load perfectly and breaking simultaneously at the full theoretical combined strength — this uneven load-sharing mechanical effect is precisely why skein strength testing (a real, standard textile testing method) typically yields somewhat LOWER results than naively multiplying single-strand tenacity by wrap count would predict.', 3, '{"skein-strength-load-sharing"}'::text[]),
('11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A yarn producer''s technical team explains ''yarn friction'' testing distinguishes ''yarn-to-metal'' friction (relevant to machine guide/needle contact, discussed earlier) from ''yarn-to-yarn'' friction (relevant to how the yarn behaves against ITSELF or adjacent yarns, e.g., during unwinding from a package or within a woven/knit structure). Why would a yarn need SEPARATE friction specifications for these two different contact scenarios rather than one single ''friction'' number?', '["Yarn-to-metal and yarn-to-yarn friction are always mathematically identical for any given yarn, making separate specification technically redundant with zero practical need for both","The two friction scenarios involve fundamentally different contact SURFACES (smooth, hard metal versus another soft, deformable yarn surface), and a yarn''s frictional behavior can differ significantly between these two contact types — a yarn engineered/finished (e.g., waxed, as discussed earlier) to have LOW yarn-to-metal friction for smooth machine processing might still show different yarn-to-yarn friction behavior (relevant to package unwinding stability or fabric structural cohesion), meaning both properties must be independently measured and specified rather than assuming one predicts the other","Yarn-to-yarn friction has no relationship to any real textile processing or product performance outcome, making this entire testing category technically unnecessary regardless of any measured difference from yarn-to-metal friction","Only yarn-to-metal friction has any practical relevance to textile manufacturing, making yarn-to-yarn friction testing a purely theoretical, practically useless measurement"]'::jsonb, 'The two friction scenarios involve fundamentally different contact SURFACES (smooth, hard metal versus another soft, deformable yarn surface), and a yarn''s frictional behavior can differ significantly between these two contact types — a yarn engineered/finished (e.g., waxed, as discussed earlier) to have LOW yarn-to-metal friction for smooth machine processing might still show different yarn-to-yarn friction behavior (relevant to package unwinding stability or fabric structural cohesion), meaning both properties must be independently measured and specified rather than assuming one predicts the other', 'This extends the earlier yarn-friction-coefficient and waxing discussions with an important surface-specificity insight — since friction fundamentally depends on the specific PAIR of contacting surfaces (not just one material''s intrinsic property alone), yarn-to-METAL friction (relevant to machine guides/needles, controllable via finishes like waxing) and yarn-to-YARN friction (relevant to package unwinding stability and fabric structural cohesion between adjacent yarns) are genuinely DIFFERENT measurable properties that don''t necessarily correlate perfectly — a yarn finish optimized to reduce metal-contact friction (for smooth machine processing) doesn''t automatically guarantee any particular yarn-to-yarn friction behavior, which is why comprehensive yarn technical specifications track both friction types separately rather than assuming a single friction measurement captures all relevant contact scenarios.', 3, '{"yarn-to-metal-vs-yarn-to-yarn-friction"}'::text[]);
COMMIT;
